WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.688 --> 00:00:02.880
Filmmakers thought the fans would never notice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.840
but they were wrong! These are the&nbsp;
sequels that ignored major cliffhangers.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:11.520
The most significant cultural impact of the&nbsp;
very long "Friday the 13th" film series is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.500
that it made homicidal maniac Jason Voorhees&nbsp;
an iconic character. He's certainly intriguing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:21.000
and mysterious, and he kills with total&nbsp;
fervor. Concealing his identity behind a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.680
creepy hockey mask, Jason looms so large that&nbsp;
it's easy to forget he's not the chief villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:31.080
of every "Friday the 13th" movie. For example,&nbsp;
his mother avenges his death in 1980's "Friday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:36.660
the 13th," and he doesn't begin his killing&nbsp;
spree until 1981's "Friday the 13th Part 2."

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:42.960
With its leading title and twist ending, 1985's&nbsp;
"Friday the 13th: A New Beginning" sets up its own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:48.240
franchise for a soft reboot. In this, the fifth&nbsp;
"Friday the 13th" movie, Jason's original killer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.040
Tommy Jarvis, remains haunted by the murder. But&nbsp;
when a Jason imposter hunts Tommy to avenge the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.240
death of his son, Tommy defeats the killer&nbsp;
and takes on the identity — and mask — of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:02.160
Jason Voorhees. This means that in the next&nbsp;
movie, "Friday the 13th Part VI: Jason Lives,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:08.220
Tommy-as-Jason is the central villain, right?&nbsp;
Nope! The 1986 sequel begins with Tommy — who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.480
played by a different actor — digging up&nbsp;
Jason's corpse to burn it. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:17.504
he accidentally resurrects the killer, and&nbsp;
with that, Jason lives to strike again.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.680
Slasher movies thrive on jump scares and sudden&nbsp;
twists. And since "I Know What You Did Last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:27.900
Summer" was something of a next-generation homage&nbsp;
to slasher flicks, the 1997 horror film made good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:32.280
on the promise of shocking and unexpected deaths.&nbsp;
One of them comes at the tail end of the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:37.200
when a character played by the most famous person&nbsp;
at the time seemingly dies in a grisly fashion.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:43.080
"He's just out there, and he's watching us&nbsp;
and waiting. WHAT ARE YOU WAITING FOR, HUH?"

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:50.340
The entire movie is about four teenagers who spend&nbsp;
a year attempting to evade death by revenge from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:55.200
a raincoat-wearing stranger they unintentionally&nbsp;
"killed" in a traffic accident. As "I Know What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.520
You Did Last Summer" reaches its conclusion, it&nbsp;
seems like Julie James, played by Jennifer Love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.440
Hewitt, is in the clear. She's left home and&nbsp;
is attending college in Boston. But when she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:09.720
heads into the shower one day, she sees "I STILL&nbsp;
KNOW" written in the steam on the shower door. Her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:14.940
unavenged victim-slash-killer shows up suddenly,&nbsp;
Julie screams, and the movie ends. At that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.060
viewers can assume the raincoat man&nbsp;
kills Julie quickly and efficiently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:23.640
but the 1998 sequel "I Still Know What You&nbsp;
Did Last Summer" begs to differ. Julie is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:27.900
main character in the movie; she apparently&nbsp;
didn't die, or even fight off her attacker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:32.580
because the film makes no mention of the&nbsp;
final, horrifying moments of the first film.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:37.920
While 2015's "Ant-Man" is more comedic and absurd&nbsp;
than a typical Marvel Cinematic Universe entry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.180
the film's stakes remain very high. Dr.&nbsp;
Hank Pym, played by Michael Douglas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:45.900
develops a suit that can shrink a human.&nbsp;
After he's kicked out of his company by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:50.400
his one-time protégé, he recruits Scott Lang&nbsp;
— played by Paul Rudd — to stop that villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.540
from figuring out the same tech and using&nbsp;
it to carry out unfathomable acts of evil.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:58.260
The whole point of "Ant-Man," then, is to&nbsp;
stop the all-important tech from getting into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:03.060
the wrong hands. But it does; a Hydra agent&nbsp;
named Mitchell Carson flees Pym Technologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:07.500
with some Yellowjacket particles and runs&nbsp;
off to parts unknown. Where does he go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:12.360
and how does he use Yellowjacket particles to&nbsp;
advance his own agenda? Audiences never find out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:16.140
because neither of the sequels — "Ant-Man&nbsp;
and the Wasp" nor "Ant-Man and the Wasp:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.580
Quantumania" — follow up on Carson. When&nbsp;
confronted with this lack of plot development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:25.920
"Ant-Man" director Peyton Reed explained the&nbsp;
unresolved cliffhanger. He told CinemaBlend,

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.040
"There was a sequence where Ant-Man&nbsp;
has an encounter with him. But then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:32.400
for a couple reasons, I felt like maybe we&nbsp;
should leave those particles out there."

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:38.040
The Marvel Cinematic Universe is a perpetually&nbsp;
complicated and intricate network of films, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:43.200
each entry directly connecting to so many others.&nbsp;
That's how Marvel Studios keeps audiences coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:48.000
back for more; we have to know what happens next,&nbsp;
particularly after the post-credits sequences.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.580
After the credits and mid-credits scenes rolled&nbsp;
on the 2016 MCU movie "Doctor Strange," viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:58.020
who stayed in their seats caught one more&nbsp;
sequence. It centers on Mordo, a supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:02.700
character who trains Dr. Stephen Strange in the&nbsp;
use and history of magic. The after-credits scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:07.860
really seems to set up Mordo, who's portrayed by&nbsp;
Academy Award-nominated actor Chiwetel Ejiofor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:12.720
for a solo movie, Disney+ TV series, or&nbsp;
significant plot arc in a future film. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.280
the sequence, Mordo discusses the overabundance&nbsp;
of sorcerers on the planet with fellow magical one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:22.200
Jonathan Pangborn. As such, Mordo presumably&nbsp;
heads out to knock off some wicked wizards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:27.000
but viewers never see that happen — not even&nbsp;
as a multiverse possibility in "Doctor Strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.120
and the Multiverse of Madness." In&nbsp;
fact, he's barely in that movie.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:35.400
In 1999, Sony bought the rights to make movies&nbsp;
based on characters in the "Spider-Man" universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:39.960
and later produced a "Spider-Man" trilogy.&nbsp;
After its rivals at Marvel Studios started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:44.700
up the Marvel Cinematic Universe, Sony ramped&nbsp;
up to stay competitive, rebooting the franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:50.100
with "The Amazing Spider-Man" in 2012 and&nbsp;
"The Amazing Spider-Man 2" in 2014. Sony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:54.480
has since released a couple of "Venom" films,&nbsp;
three more "Spider-Man" movies, and "Morbius,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:59.040
but the company never made a project featuring&nbsp;
the Sinister Six — a team-up of the biggest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.780
baddest, and most famous villains from the&nbsp;
decades-old property. Among the antagonists:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:09.660
Doctor Octopus, Electro, Mysterio,&nbsp;
Sandman, Vulture, and Kraven the Hunter.

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:13.680
But it looked like Sony had a "Sinister&nbsp;
Six" movie in the works around 2014,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:17.880
which it later confirmed, according to&nbsp;
Deadline. A sequence in "The Amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:22.320
Spider-Man 2" offers a quick glimpse of costume&nbsp;
and prop elements of Sinister Six members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:29.820
such as Vulture's wings and Doctor Octopus'&nbsp;
tentacles. While some of those villains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:33.420
turn up in later Spider-Man movies,&nbsp;
starting with the MCU-based "Spider-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:38.492
Homecoming," the "Sinister Six" movie that was&nbsp;
so blatantly promised never came to fruition.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:42.720
In "Pirates of the Caribbean: On Stranger&nbsp;
Tides," rakish and roguish pirate Captain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:46.860
Jack Sparrow finally seems to have met&nbsp;
both his romantic and adversarial match&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:52.200
in Angelica — played by Penélope Cruz. She's&nbsp;
a pirate as crafty and daring as Captain Jack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:56.400
and she claims to be the daughter of the&nbsp;
legendary Blackbeard. Oscar-winner Cruz came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.080
on board the "Pirates of the Caribbean" franchise&nbsp;
nearly a decade in and injected new energy into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:05.820
it. But "On Stranger Tides" would mark her, and&nbsp;
Angelica's, one and only appearance in the series.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:11.220
Growing weary of Angelica's machinations and&nbsp;
unwilling to trust her, Jack abandons her on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:15.540
a tiny island before returning to his life&nbsp;
of independent piracy and heading off to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:20.400
his lost ship. That's how the movie ends, but a&nbsp;
post-credits scene shows audiences what happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.560
next. A voodoo doll that looks like Captain Jack&nbsp;
washes up on Angelica's island, and it's evident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:30.360
she plans to use the darkly magical object to&nbsp;
exact revenge on him. Jack Sparrow dealing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:34.380
a voodoo curse should have been a big part of&nbsp;
the plot of 2017's "Pirates of the Caribbean:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:40.020
Dead Men Tell No Tales," but it isn't.&nbsp;
Angelica doesn't even show up in that sequel.

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:45.840
"Prometheus," a prequel to the "Alien" series, is&nbsp;
slow, cerebral, and requires intense concentration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:51.480
from audiences. Ridley Scott, who directed the&nbsp;
initial "Alien" in 1979, helmed the 2012 movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:56.160
which purported to set up the events that lead&nbsp;
to those other blockbusters. In the film, two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:02.040
scientists lead a deep-space expedition to explore&nbsp;
humanity's possible extraterrestrial origins. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:06.360
just like in the other "Alien" movies, there's&nbsp;
a lot of foreboding, death, and destruction.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:08.760
"Prometheus" winds up with optimistic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:12.600
spiritual scientist Elizabeth Shaw as&nbsp;
the only human survivor on her ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:17.340
along with an android named David. At the end&nbsp;
of the film, Shaw and David seek out the home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:21.480
planet of the menacing Engineers to discover&nbsp;
why they wanted to exterminate all humans.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:25.440
A sequel to "Prometheus," "Alien:&nbsp;
Covenant" hit theaters in 2017.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:28.920
Shaw only briefly appears as a&nbsp;
corpse experimented on by David,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:33.780
who also destroyed the evil alien threat from&nbsp;
the first movie. Shaw and David's interactions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:38.280
with the Engineers are not discussed, and&nbsp;
so their motivations remain a mystery.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:42.600
The 2000s action movie franchise "xXx"&nbsp;
positioned itself as an edgier James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:46.920
Bond series for a new generation. Vin&nbsp;
Diesel stars as Xander "XXX" Cage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:51.960
an extreme sports athlete who cheats death as&nbsp;
a matter of course and is recruited by the NSA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:56.460
to be a top spy. Unlike the ongoing Bond&nbsp;
franchise, which is primarily a string of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:01.020
self-contained movies, the three "xXx" films&nbsp;
all follow a sequence of events. That makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:04.920
the filmmakers' decision to ignore some&nbsp;
cliffhangers very confusing to viewers.

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:10.800
At the end of 2002's "xXx," Xander Cage completes&nbsp;
his mission and is enjoying some well-earned rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:15.660
and relaxation when handlers approach him with&nbsp;
his next big spy assignment. As the film ends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:20.040
it's unclear if he's going to accept the job, and&nbsp;
nobody ever finds out, because Diesel didn't want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:25.860
to return for 2005's "xXx: State of the Union."&nbsp;
The story goes that Xander Cage died offscreen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:31.260
while Ice Cube plays a new agent named Darius&nbsp;
Stone. But then Diesel returned in 2017 for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:36.420
the third film, "xXx: Return of Xander Cage" —&nbsp;
meaning the character never really died after all.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:40.920
Since launching in 1996, the "Mission:&nbsp;
Impossible" franchise has become one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:45.540
of the most successful action series of&nbsp;
the past 30 years. 2011 saw the fourth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:48.420
entry of the franchise arrive&nbsp;
in theaters, titled "Mission:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:53.160
Impossible — Ghost Protocol." It introduced&nbsp;
a new team of IMF agents who are working with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:57.360
Ethan Hunt to try to clear their names after they&nbsp;
are accused of planting a bomb in the Kremlin.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:01.020
These fresh additions to the series&nbsp;
included Jeremy Renner's intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:04.560
analyst William Brandt, Simon&nbsp;
Pegg's field agent Benji Dunn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:08.880
and Paula Patton's agent Jane Carter.&nbsp;
After they are successful in their mission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:13.140
Hunt gathers everyone together again at the&nbsp;
end of the film and hands them another one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:17.880
implying that this group will continue to work&nbsp;
together as a new terrorist organization emerges.

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:22.500
Unfortunately, fans never got to see any more&nbsp;
of Carter despite the fact that other characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:26.640
from the film reappeared in subsequent&nbsp;
releases. With little mention of her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:30.300
it is unclear where she is or what might&nbsp;
have happened. Carter was seemingly set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:33.720
to return to the franchise, with rumors&nbsp;
suggesting that she would have appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:37.620
in "Rogue Nation," but scheduling conflicts&nbsp;
meant her character was cut from the film.

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:42.960
Before the Marvel Cinematic Universe became the&nbsp;
juggernaut it is, Marvel Comics fans had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:47.520
"X-Men" series to keep them occupied. Beginning&nbsp;
with the first movie in 2000, the franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:53.160
ballooned to more than a dozen films. 2006 saw the&nbsp;
release of the third movie in the series, "X-Men:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:57.180
The Last Stand." It follows Magneto and his&nbsp;
Brotherhood of Mutants as they attempt to prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:02.460
a cure for the mutant gene from being forcefully&nbsp;
imposed on mutantkind. It also features Jean Grey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:07.860
unleashing the Phoenix Force, ultimately killing&nbsp;
Professor Charles Xavier and allying with Magneto.

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:12.420
The movie ends with a post-credit scene where a&nbsp;
coma patient in a hospital wakes up and talks with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:17.100
the voice of Charles Xavier. This suggests that&nbsp;
Professor X has somehow survived by placing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:22.080
mind into the body of another person. But Charles&nbsp;
Xavier, played by Patrick Stewart, returns in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:27.060
three sequel films. He first makes a cameo in&nbsp;
the 2013 film "The Wolverine" before becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:33.060
part of the main cast again in the 2014 release&nbsp;
"X-Men: Days of Future Past" and 2017's "Logan."

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:36.660
It isn't clear how Professor X was&nbsp;
able to return to his normal body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:42.300
as it had apparently been disintegrated by Jean&nbsp;
Grey. No explanation is given either. He simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:46.500
tells Wolverine that he too has gifts, but he&nbsp;
doesn't reveal what happened between the films.

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:48.600
"How's this possible?"

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:54.960
"As I told you a long time ago,&nbsp;
you're not the only one with gifts."

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:58.980
One of the defining aspects of the James Bond&nbsp;
movies is that different actors have played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:03.960
007 over the years. Of course, this tradition&nbsp;
only arose because original actor Sean Connery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:08.880
left the franchise. When he departed following&nbsp;
the release of "You Only Live Twice" in 1967,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:14.280
George Lazenby took on the role of the fictional&nbsp;
spy in 1969's "On Her Majesty's Secret Service."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:19.080
The latter film follows Bond as he tries to stop&nbsp;
Blofeld from destroying the world's food supply.

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:24.540
By the end of the movie, Bond has fallen in&nbsp;
love with Contessa Teresa "Tracy" di Vicenzo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:27.420
portrayed by Diana Rigg. The two eventually marry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:31.320
though the relationship is cut short when&nbsp;
they're involved in a drive-by shooting at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:36.900
the hands of Blofeld and his henchwoman. Tracy is&nbsp;
killed and Bond is left devastated by the events.

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:41.700
Producers were able to convince Connery to return&nbsp;
for "Diamonds Are Forever," and the opening scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:45.840
shows Bond tracking down and killing who&nbsp;
he believes to be the real Blofeld. Yet no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:50.460
explanation is given as to why he is so determined&nbsp;
to dispatch the villain and Tracy isn't mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:54.360
at any point in the film. In fact, there are&nbsp;
some suggestions in the opening moments that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:58.712
Bond is actually pursuing Blofeld directly&nbsp;
after the events of "You Only Live Twice."

